WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.453
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4514.

2
00:00:01.453 --> 00:00:02.905
Begin Session II.]

3
00:00:02.905 --> 00:00:06.295
<v JAMIE DIGILORMO>Okay, so we are here today, Friday.

4
00:00:06.295 --> 00:00:08.716
It is September the fourth?

5
00:00:08.716 --> 00:00:10.653
The fourth or fifth?

6
00:00:10.653 --> 00:00:11.137
Fourth.

7
00:00:11.137 --> 00:00:13.074
So at two o'clock.

8
00:00:13.074 --> 00:00:18.400
We're still here on 1606 Opelousas Street in Lake Charles, Louisiana.

9
00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:26.147
I'm Jamie Digilormo, and I'm sitting here with Mr. Wes Montgomery doing a follow up interview.

10
00:00:26.147 --> 00:00:29.053
Chelsea Arseneault is our audio engineer.

11
00:00:29.053 --> 00:00:37.284
Last time we started talking about your childhood over in Duson and Crowley [Louisiana] and that area,

12
00:00:37.284 --> 00:00:46.000
but I kind of wanted to go back a little further and find out about your grandparents.

13
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:46.515
<v WESLEY MONTGOMERY>Yeah.

14
00:00:46.515 --> 00:00:51.146
<v DIGILORMO>Let's make sure this . . . there . . . Pause for a second.

15
00:00:51.146 --> 00:00:53.204
Fix this . . . Continue recording.

16
00:00:53.204 --> 00:00:59.893
So I wanted to talk to you about your grandparents a little bit.

17
00:00:59.893 --> 00:01:03.495
We didn't talk at all about that.

18
00:01:03.495 --> 00:01:05.553
Where they came from.

19
00:01:05.553 --> 00:01:08.126
What about your daddy's side?

20
00:01:08.126 --> 00:01:17.903
<v MONTGOMERY>On my daddy's side . . . Now my daddy you have to understand, he had a gift from Jesus from heaven.

21
00:01:17.903 --> 00:01:20.476
He was a faith healer.

22
00:01:20.476 --> 00:01:21.505
<v DIGILORMO>Yes, sir.

23
00:01:21.505 --> 00:01:22.019
We . . .

24
00:01:22.019 --> 00:01:24.078
<v MONTGOMERY>He could cure you.

25
00:01:24.078 --> 00:01:28.194
He'd go get them out of the hospital.

26
00:01:28.194 --> 00:01:32.825
God was in him, and he was in God.

27
00:01:32.825 --> 00:01:37.971
He was . . . I mean, Billy Graham couldn't touch my daddy.

28
00:01:37.971 --> 00:01:39.000
<v DIGILORMO>[Laughs]

29
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:41.818
<v MONTGOMERY>My daddy healed people.

30
00:01:41.818 --> 00:01:50.273
He had white folks . . . He had about four white to one black.

31
00:01:50.273 --> 00:01:55.205
That's how much white people he had.

32
00:01:55.205 --> 00:01:56.614
Every day.

33
00:01:56.614 --> 00:01:59.432
Seven days a week.

34
00:01:59.432 --> 00:02:02.250
He was a healer.

35
00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:07.886
<v DIGILORMO>And did he get that from his father?

36
00:02:07.886 --> 00:02:08.591
<v MONTGOMERY>Yeah.

37
00:02:08.591 --> 00:02:10.000
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

38
00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:12.933
<v MONTGOMERY>His daddy give it to him.

39
00:02:12.933 --> 00:02:14.889
<v DIGILORMO>Where was he from?

40
00:02:14.889 --> 00:02:15.378
<v MONTGOMERY>Duson.

41
00:02:15.378 --> 00:02:16.844
All from Duson.

42
00:02:16.844 --> 00:02:18.311
<v DIGILORMO>All from Duson?

43
00:02:18.311 --> 00:02:18.800
Okay.

44
00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:19.289
<v MONTGOMERY>Yeah.

45
00:02:19.289 --> 00:02:29.067
My daddy he had . . . At one time when I was living in California, had ten children living in Los Angeles.

46
00:02:29.067 --> 00:02:38.356
So he'd go to California and treat the people three times a year, then he’d come back to Duson.

47
00:02:38.356 --> 00:02:43.733
But he’d go to California just for three times a year.

48
00:02:43.733 --> 00:02:49.600
Stay there a month, a few months, then he'd come back here.

49
00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:54.000
<v DIGILORMO>And he treated people in Duson as well?

50
00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:57.207
<v MONTGOMERY>Oh yeah, he'd stop in to Houston.

51
00:02:57.207 --> 00:03:03.622
He'd go to the Hilton and get the biggest suite they had. [?] every day.

52
00:03:03.622 --> 00:03:09.578
He’d stay there for maybe five, six weeks, treating people in Houston, Texas.

53
00:03:09.578 --> 00:03:12.785
He was really a man of God.

54
00:03:12.785 --> 00:03:15.076
God hear me speaking now.

55
00:03:15.076 --> 00:03:18.283
So I was working for Conoco refinery.

56
00:03:18.283 --> 00:03:22.407
I’d been there since I was twenty years old.

57
00:03:22.407 --> 00:03:27.447
And my daddy called me and asked me to come home.

58
00:03:27.447 --> 00:03:30.196
I didn't know what he wanted.

59
00:03:30.196 --> 00:03:34.319
"Wes,” he said, “I need you to come home."

60
00:03:34.319 --> 00:03:36.610
When my daddy spoke [F.

61
00:03:36.610 --> 00:03:37.985
E. Hunton?] spoke.

62
00:03:37.985 --> 00:03:40.734
When my daddy spoke we listened.

63
00:03:40.734 --> 00:03:43.483
I could get fifteen, twenty children.

64
00:03:43.483 --> 00:03:45.316
That didn't mean nothing.

65
00:03:45.316 --> 00:03:47.606
When he spoke you listened.

66
00:03:47.606 --> 00:03:50.814
"Wes, I need you to come home."

67
00:03:50.814 --> 00:03:56.312
So when I got off from Conoco refinery I told my wife.

68
00:03:56.312 --> 00:03:58.603
I said, "My daddy called.

69
00:03:58.603 --> 00:04:00.435
Get the children ready."

70
00:04:00.435 --> 00:04:02.268
Our four little children.

71
00:04:02.268 --> 00:04:04.559
So my wife got ready.

72
00:04:04.559 --> 00:04:06.850
<v DIGILORMO>That was your wife Agnes?

73
00:04:06.850 --> 00:04:07.766
<v MONTGOMERY>Agnes, Agnes.

74
00:04:07.766 --> 00:04:09.141
So we left.

75
00:04:09.141 --> 00:04:13.722
We went to Duson to my daddy and my mama.

76
00:04:13.722 --> 00:04:21.053
We got there about, I don't know, I guess around nine o'clock that morning . . . Ten o'clock.

77
00:04:21.053 --> 00:04:29.300
When I got there he was . . . He had a room where he'd treat people like this size here.

78
00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:30.217
Then he

79
00:04:30.217 --> 00:04:33.882
<c.p>had another room smaller where he'd just pray.

80
00:04:33.882 --> 00:04:36.173
You couldn't go in there.

81
00:04:36.173 --> 00:04:38.006
Nobody go in there.

82
00:04:38.006 --> 00:04:39.838
He just go pray.

83
00:04:39.838 --> 00:04:48.086
So we walked into that treating room, and Daddy he had all of these white folks in there.

84
00:04:48.086 --> 00:04:49.460
Nothing but white.

85
00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:54.958
He grabbed his four little grandchildren, and he kissed all of them.

86
00:04:54.958 --> 00:04:58.165
He grabbed my wife, he kissed Agnes.

87
00:04:58.165 --> 00:05:00.914
Then he grabbed and kissed me.

88
00:05:00.914 --> 00:05:07.329
So he told me, "Wes you go in the kitchen and meet your mother."

89
00:05:07.329 --> 00:05:12.827
And he said, "I'll be there when I clear all the people.”

90
00:05:12.827 --> 00:05:16.034
He had so many calls out there.

91
00:05:16.034 --> 00:05:21.990
The whole . . . everything was packed outside the house with calls, inside the house.

92
00:05:21.990 --> 00:05:25.198
And he said, “I’ll come meet you."

93
00:05:25.198 --> 00:05:28.863
Sure enough around eleven, eleven-thirty, clear everybody out.

94
00:05:28.863 --> 00:05:32.529
He come sit with us in the kitchen.

95
00:05:32.529 --> 00:05:38.027
He told me, he said, "Wesley, I need to talk to you."

96
00:05:38.027 --> 00:05:40.776
"Dad," I said, "What you got?"

97
00:05:40.776 --> 00:05:46.732
He said, "All of my children in California, they getting on my time.

98
00:05:46.732 --> 00:05:49.023
They say I'm getting old.

99
00:05:49.023 --> 00:05:52.230
They want me to pass my treatment."

100
00:05:52.230 --> 00:05:54.979
He got it from his daddy.

101
00:05:54.979 --> 00:05:58.644
I told him, "I can't live in California.

102
00:05:58.644 --> 00:06:02.768
I want to live and treat people in Duson."

103
00:06:02.768 --> 00:06:06.892
So he said, "That's what I called you for."

104
00:06:06.892 --> 00:06:12.390
He said, "Look,” he said, “Would you like to be a treater?"

105
00:06:12.390 --> 00:06:14.222
I said, "Yes, Dad."

106
00:06:14.222 --> 00:06:19.262
Well he said, "My first charge was your baby sister Diane."

107
00:06:19.262 --> 00:06:20.637
My baby sister.

108
00:06:20.637 --> 00:06:25.219
He said, "I was going to give her the treatment.

109
00:06:25.219 --> 00:06:26.593
She wanted it.

110
00:06:26.593 --> 00:06:27.968
She'd accept it."

111
00:06:27.968 --> 00:06:36.215
But he said, “Then I asked her ‘can you deal with the spirit?' She said, ‘Oh no Dad.

112
00:06:36.215 --> 00:06:40.797
Oh no.’ She said, ‘I can’t deal with the spirit.’”

113
00:06:40.797 --> 00:06:44.462
Well he say, "You can't be a treater."

114
00:06:44.462 --> 00:06:48.586
So my daddy, see how we talking in here?

115
00:06:48.586 --> 00:06:54.084
My daddy talk to the spirit like I'm talking to you all!

116
00:06:54.084 --> 00:06:55.000
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

117
00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:56.500
<v MONTGOMERY>Well this man was something else.

118
00:06:56.500 --> 00:06:59.000
It was something that you never saw in your life.

119
00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:01.000
<v DIGILORMO>And his daddy could do it, too?

120
00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:01.451
<v MONTGOMERY>Yeah!

121
00:07:01.451 --> 00:07:04.159
That's where he got it from.

122
00:07:04.159 --> 00:07:07.768
<v DIGLIORMO>Did your grandfather get it from his daddy?

123
00:07:07.768 --> 00:07:09.122
<v MONTGOMERY>I don't know.

124
00:07:09.122 --> 00:07:10.476
<v DIGLIORMO>You don't know?

125
00:07:10.476 --> 00:07:12.280
<v MONTGOMERY>I don't know that.

126
00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:12.732
<v DIGLIORMO>Okay.

127
00:07:12.732 --> 00:07:16.793
<v MONTGOMERY>But I know he got it from his daddy.

128
00:07:16.793 --> 00:07:21.756
I would assume he got it from his daddy, my grandfather.

129
00:07:21.756 --> 00:07:24.012
But I can't quote that.

130
00:07:24.012 --> 00:07:24.915
<v DIGLIORMO>Right, right.

131
00:07:24.915 --> 00:07:28.524
<v MONTGOMERY>I know where my daddy got it from.

132
00:07:28.524 --> 00:07:33.037
So he said, "Wes, Diane can't deal with the spirit.

133
00:07:33.037 --> 00:07:34.841
She can't handle it.

134
00:07:34.841 --> 00:07:37.098
You was my second choice."

135
00:07:37.098 --> 00:07:41.159
He said, "Do you want to be a treater?”

136
00:07:41.159 --> 00:07:42.963
I said, “Yes, Dad.”

137
00:07:42.963 --> 00:07:46.573
He said, “Can you deal with the spirit?"

138
00:07:46.573 --> 00:07:47.927
I said, "Today."

139
00:07:47.927 --> 00:07:49.280
He said, "What?"

140
00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:50.634
I said, "Today.

141
00:07:50.634 --> 00:07:52.439
I'm ready right now."

142
00:07:52.439 --> 00:07:55.598
So this is what he told me.

143
00:07:55.598 --> 00:07:57.854
And God is listening to

144
00:07:57.854 --> 00:08:01.012
<c.p>me right now, what I'm telling you.

145
00:08:01.012 --> 00:08:03.268
You see, God hears everything.

146
00:08:03.268 --> 00:08:05.524
I'm telling you the truth.

147
00:08:05.524 --> 00:08:08.232
I told him I was ready.

148
00:08:08.232 --> 00:08:15.451
Well he said, "I'll tell you what, I'm not ready to give it to you today."

149
00:08:15.451 --> 00:08:20.866
But he say, "You will be the next treater in the family."

150
00:08:20.866 --> 00:08:30.793
And he says, "I'm going to give it to you," but he said, "I'm not ready today to pass it on down.”

151
00:08:30.793 --> 00:08:32.598
Which I understood that.

152
00:08:32.598 --> 00:08:35.305
Because he caught me by surprise.

153
00:08:35.305 --> 00:08:37.561
God know I was ready.

154
00:08:37.561 --> 00:08:39.366
So it went on.

155
00:08:39.366 --> 00:08:43.427
It went on, and then he died in California.

156
00:08:43.427 --> 00:08:45.683
Took sick in Los Angeles.

157
00:08:45.683 --> 00:08:47.939
He died at the hospital.

158
00:08:47.939 --> 00:08:50.195
I didn't go to California.

159
00:08:50.195 --> 00:08:56.963
I should have gone, but I guess God knew I was not supposed to go.

160
00:08:56.963 --> 00:09:01.927
So I did not go to California when he was sick.

161
00:09:01.927 --> 00:09:06.890
So my brother, the one named [Oren?], is older than me.

162
00:09:06.890 --> 00:09:10.500
He died there about seven or eight years.

163
00:09:10.500 --> 00:09:15.012
He told me, "Wes,” he say, “You missed Dad's treatment."

164
00:09:15.012 --> 00:09:16.366
I said, "Yes."

165
00:09:16.366 --> 00:09:18.622
He said, "You wasn't there."

166
00:09:18.622 --> 00:09:22.683
I said, "No." But he said, "I got it."

167
00:09:22.683 --> 00:09:25.841
So I believed him, but he lied.

168
00:09:25.841 --> 00:09:29.000
<v DIGLIORMO>How did you know he lied?

169
00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:31.702
<v MONTGOMERY>Walter, my other brother, we like twins.

170
00:09:31.702 --> 00:09:32.088
<v DIGLIORMO>[Agrees]

171
00:09:32.088 --> 00:09:33.632
<v MONTGOMERY>I'm eighty-one, he's eighty-two.

172
00:09:33.632 --> 00:09:36.719
My mama raised us two at a time.

173
00:09:36.719 --> 00:09:37.877
She had sixteen.

174
00:09:37.877 --> 00:09:40.965
She raised us all two at a time.

175
00:09:40.965 --> 00:09:43.667
So me and Walter raised like twins.

176
00:09:43.667 --> 00:09:45.982
All the Montgomerys love to drink.

177
00:09:45.982 --> 00:09:47.526
They love to party.

178
00:09:47.526 --> 00:09:48.684
That's the Montgomerys.

179
00:09:48.684 --> 00:09:49.070
<v DIGILORMO>[Laughs]

180
00:09:49.070 --> 00:09:51.772
<v MONTOGMERY>He come out here for a vacation.

181
00:09:51.772 --> 00:09:58.333
We went out in the night club having a good time, and we brought my daddy up.

182
00:09:58.333 --> 00:10:01.421
Daddy was a big thing in our life.

183
00:10:01.421 --> 00:10:07.211
My mother too, but my daddy played one heck of a role in our lives.

184
00:10:07.211 --> 00:10:07.596
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

185
00:10:07.596 --> 00:10:10.684
<v MONTGOMERY>So "Wes," he said, "you missed Dad's treatment."

186
00:10:10.684 --> 00:10:14.544
I said, "Yeah,” but I say, we all got nickname.

187
00:10:14.544 --> 00:10:16.474
I said, "Stick got it."

188
00:10:16.474 --> 00:10:18.404
Oren, we call him Stick.

189
00:10:18.404 --> 00:10:20.333
He said, "Who told you?"

190
00:10:20.333 --> 00:10:22.649
I said, "Stick told me that."

191
00:10:22.649 --> 00:10:30.754
He said, "When Dad died," he said, "just me and Stick was at the bedside when he gave up his ghost."

192
00:10:30.754 --> 00:10:33.070
He said, "He lied to you."

193
00:10:33.070 --> 00:10:35.000
I said, "What you mean?"

194
00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:38.088
He said, "He ain’t told you the truth.”

195
00:10:38.088 --> 00:10:41.175
He said, “He asked Dad for the treatment.

196
00:10:41.175 --> 00:10:42.719
I was right there.

197
00:10:42.719 --> 00:10:43.877
Dad never spoke.

198
00:10:43.877 --> 00:10:46.579
He just did this with his head.

199
00:10:46.579 --> 00:10:52.754
He came back the second time with our Dad for the treatment, because he wanted it.

200
00:10:52.754 --> 00:10:54.298
Daddy shook his head.

201
00:10:54.298 --> 00:10:57.000
Then he died [break in tape].

202
00:10:57.000 --> 00:10:58.235
<v DIGILORMO>Testing one two.

203
00:10:58.235 --> 00:11:00.706
Say . . . Say your name for us?

204
00:11:00.706 --> 00:11:06.059
To make . . . Okay so we were talking about your dad and his dad.

205
00:11:06.059 --> 00:11:06.471
<v MONTGOMERY>Yeah.

206
00:11:06.471 --> 00:11:10.176
<v DIGILORMO>And how his dad was a treater as well.

207
00:11:10.176 --> 00:11:11.000
<v MONTGOMERY>Yeah.

208
00:11:11.000 --> 00:11:11.875
<v DIGILORMO>In Duson.

209
00:11:11.875 --> 00:11:12.312
<v MONTGOMERY>Yeah.

210
00:11:12.312 --> 00:11:15.375
<v DIGILORMO>What did he do for a living?

211
00:11:15.375 --> 00:11:16.688
<v MONTGOMERY>Who, my dad?

212
00:11:16.688 --> 00:11:18.000
<v DIGILORMO>Your grandfather.

213
00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:21.600
<v MONTGOMERY>Well he was a . . . I never met my grandfather.

214
00:11:21.600 --> 00:11:22.000
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

215
00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:24.400
<v MONTGOMERY>You know I never met him.

216
00:11:24.400 --> 00:11:26.000
I met my grandmother.

217
00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:26.400
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

218
00:11:26.400 --> 00:11:28.400
<v MONTGOMERY>But I never met him.

219
00:11:28.400 --> 00:11:30.400
I wished I would have.

220
00:11:30.400 --> 00:11:33.200
Bad, I want to meet my grandfather.

221
00:11:33.200 --> 00:11:35.200
But I met my grandmother.

222
00:11:35.200 --> 00:11:36.000
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

223
00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:37.105
<v MONTGOMERY>Little short woman.

224
00:11:37.105 --> 00:11:42.632
So he must have been a tall man, because my grandmother was about your height.

225
00:11:42.632 --> 00:11:43.000
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

226
00:11:43.000 --> 00:11:43.737
<v MONTGOMERY>Short woman.

227
00:11:43.737 --> 00:11:46.684
<v DIGILORMO>So you don't know what he did or . . .?

228
00:11:46.684 --> 00:11:48.895
<v MONTGOMERY>He had to be a farmer.

229
00:11:48.895 --> 00:11:49.263
<v DIGILORMO>Farmer.

230
00:11:49.263 --> 00:11:50.000
Yeah.

231
00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:50.365
<v MONTGOMERY>Crop.

232
00:11:50.365 --> 00:11:51.827
Sweet potato, corn, cotton.

233
00:11:51.827 --> 00:11:54.019
That's . . . Well that's what they did.

234
00:11:54.019 --> 00:11:54.385
<v DIGILORMO>Right.

235
00:11:54.385 --> 00:11:55.846
<v MONTGOMERY>That was they living.

236
00:11:55.846 --> 00:11:57.673
<v DIGILORMO>What did your grandmother do?

237
00:11:57.673 --> 00:11:58.769
Do you know?

238
00:11:58.769 --> 00:11:59.500
Did she . . . ?

239
00:11:59.500 --> 00:12:04.615
<v MONTGOMERY>When I met her she was living with her daughter, my daddy's sister at

240
00:12:04.615 --> 00:12:05.346
<c.p>Newton [Louisiana].

241
00:12:05.346 --> 00:12:08.269
She was living there when I met her.

242
00:12:08.269 --> 00:12:09.000
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

243
00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:10.895
<v MONTGOMERY>Little short woman wearing a bonnet.

244
00:12:10.895 --> 00:12:11.526
You know?

245
00:12:11.526 --> 00:12:11.842
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

246
00:12:11.842 --> 00:12:13.421
<v MONTGOMERY>I loved her to death.

247
00:12:13.421 --> 00:12:16.579
And as far as I know that's all she did.

248
00:12:16.579 --> 00:12:16.895
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

249
00:12:16.895 --> 00:12:18.789
<v MONTGOMERY>She was staying with her daughter.

250
00:12:18.789 --> 00:12:20.684
Had chicken, and geese, and ducks.

251
00:12:20.684 --> 00:12:22.895
Had all of them in the yard.

252
00:12:22.895 --> 00:12:24.789
<v DIGILORMO>Okay, so she raised . . . She stayed . . .

253
00:12:24.789 --> 00:12:25.421
<v MONTGOMERY>Oh, yeah.

254
00:12:25.421 --> 00:12:27.000
<v DIGILORMO>Home and raised animals.

255
00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:29.838
<v MONTGOMERY>Oh, yeah, oh, yeah, she stayed home.

256
00:12:29.838 --> 00:12:32.676
<v DIGILORMO>She had herself a garden, I imagine?

257
00:12:32.676 --> 00:12:33.486
<v MONTGOMERY>Not her.

258
00:12:33.486 --> 00:12:34.297
<v DIGILORMO>Not her?

259
00:12:34.297 --> 00:12:34.703
Okay.

260
00:12:34.703 --> 00:12:38.351
<v MONTGOMERY>But my auntie, her daughter, and [?] my daddy's sister.

261
00:12:38.351 --> 00:12:39.162
<v DIGILORMO>Got you.

262
00:12:39.162 --> 00:12:41.189
<v MONTGOMERY>She did all of that.

263
00:12:41.189 --> 00:12:42.000
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

264
00:12:42.000 --> 00:12:42.371
<v MONTGOMERY>Yes.

265
00:12:42.371 --> 00:12:43.114
<v DIGILORMO>That's beautiful.

266
00:12:43.114 --> 00:12:43.857
<v MONTGOMERY>Oh, yeah.

267
00:12:43.857 --> 00:12:47.200
<v DIGILORMO>So what about your mother's side of the family?

268
00:12:47.200 --> 00:12:50.171
Did you met your grandparents on her side?

269
00:12:50.171 --> 00:12:54.257
<v MONTGOMERY>No, I didn't met none of them on my mother's side.

270
00:12:54.257 --> 00:12:55.000
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

271
00:12:55.000 --> 00:12:56.385
<v MONTGOMERY>Just my daddy's side.

272
00:12:56.385 --> 00:12:57.769
I met my grandmother.

273
00:12:57.769 --> 00:13:02.962
But on my mother's side . . . Good Lord in heaven, how beautiful would that have been.

274
00:13:02.962 --> 00:13:05.731
I never met my grandfather or my grandmother.

275
00:13:05.731 --> 00:13:10.577
I just met all of the brothers and the sisters . . . Mama's brothers and sisters.

276
00:13:10.577 --> 00:13:15.077
But I just . . . They passed away before I was able to meet them.

277
00:13:15.077 --> 00:13:15.423
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

278
00:13:15.423 --> 00:13:15.769
Right.

279
00:13:15.769 --> 00:13:16.462
<v MONTGOMERY>You know?

280
00:13:16.462 --> 00:13:18.885
<v DIGILORMO>And were they from Duson as well?

281
00:13:18.885 --> 00:13:20.962
<v MONTGOMERY>No, they were from Opelousas [Louisiana].

282
00:13:20.962 --> 00:13:21.308
<v DIGILORMO>Opelousas.

283
00:13:21.308 --> 00:13:22.000
Okay.

284
00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:22.700
<v MONTGOMERY>Sunset, Opelousas.

285
00:13:22.700 --> 00:13:25.150
That was back there in the country.

286
00:13:25.150 --> 00:13:25.850
You know?

287
00:13:25.850 --> 00:13:26.200
<v DIGILORMO>Right.

288
00:13:26.200 --> 00:13:26.550
Yeah.

289
00:13:26.550 --> 00:13:26.900
<v MONTGOMERY>Yeah.

290
00:13:26.900 --> 00:13:27.250
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

291
00:13:27.250 --> 00:13:30.750
And do you have any that . . . Where they croppers too?

292
00:13:30.750 --> 00:13:32.500
I mean, did they grow . . .?

293
00:13:32.500 --> 00:13:32.850
<v MONTGOMERY>Yeah.

294
00:13:32.850 --> 00:13:33.200
That's . . .

295
00:13:33.200 --> 00:13:33.550
<v DIGILORMO>Farmers.

296
00:13:33.550 --> 00:13:34.950
<v MONTGOMERY>. . . all you can do.

297
00:13:34.950 --> 00:13:36.000
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah, right.

298
00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:40.971
<v MONTGOMERY>Back then black folks . . . Really that's . . . I'm telling you like it is.

299
00:13:40.971 --> 00:13:45.529
What black folks did in them days was . . . They were farmers.

300
00:13:45.529 --> 00:13:46.771
Cotton, corn, potatoes.

301
00:13:46.771 --> 00:13:47.186
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

302
00:13:47.186 --> 00:13:49.257
<v MONTGOMERY>That's what they could do.

303
00:13:49.257 --> 00:13:52.157
You weren’t allowed to do anything else.

304
00:13:52.157 --> 00:13:52.571
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

305
00:13:52.571 --> 00:13:55.057
<v MONTGOMERY>Today black people runs the world.

306
00:13:55.057 --> 00:13:55.886
Look Obama.

307
00:13:55.886 --> 00:13:57.543
They runs the world

308
00:13:57.543 --> 00:13:57.957
<c.p>today.

309
00:13:57.957 --> 00:14:04.171
Back then they couldn't do nothing but clean commode, clean your floor, clean your house.

310
00:14:04.171 --> 00:14:05.000
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

311
00:14:05.000 --> 00:14:08.200
<v MONTGOMERY>That was the way of life.

312
00:14:08.200 --> 00:14:08.733
<v DIGILORMO>Right.

313
00:14:08.733 --> 00:14:10.333
<v MONTGOMERY>Let's face it.

314
00:14:10.333 --> 00:14:11.933
That was it.

315
00:14:11.933 --> 00:14:14.600
And happy to do that.

316
00:14:14.600 --> 00:14:15.133
<v DIGILORMO>Right.

317
00:14:15.133 --> 00:14:18.333
<v MONTGOMERY>That was the way of life.

318
00:14:18.333 --> 00:14:20.467
And I accepted it.

319
00:14:20.467 --> 00:14:22.067
I didn't fight.

320
00:14:22.067 --> 00:14:22.600
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

321
00:14:22.600 --> 00:14:35.400
<v MONTGOMERY>And to get back on my brother, Walter when he told me about my brother Oren hadn't told me the truth about my daddy.

322
00:14:35.400 --> 00:14:38.067
That really got to me.

323
00:14:38.067 --> 00:14:44.467
So we was out at the night club; just partying, two brothers.

324
00:14:44.467 --> 00:14:47.667
We called ourselves . . . We were twins.

325
00:14:47.667 --> 00:14:51.400
Like right now I’m eighty-one, he’s eighty-two.

326
00:14:51.400 --> 00:14:59.400
And I said, "Well Walter, my daddy called me and asked me to come home.

327
00:14:59.400 --> 00:15:03.667
He was going to give me the treatment.

328
00:15:03.667 --> 00:15:04.733
Called me."

329
00:15:04.733 --> 00:15:09.533
And I says, "I wasn't there when he died."

330
00:15:09.533 --> 00:15:16.467
And see, my daddy's sister in Newton, the one where my grandmother was

331
00:15:16.467 --> 00:15:25.000
<c.p>staying with her, she told me, said, "When Abraham died he knew he wasn't coming back."

332
00:15:25.000 --> 00:15:26.600
I said, "What?"

333
00:15:26.600 --> 00:15:31.400
Say, "Abraham knew he wasn't coming back to Duson.

334
00:15:31.400 --> 00:15:37.800
He know when he died he was not going to be back."

335
00:15:37.800 --> 00:15:39.933
I didn't know that.

336
00:15:39.933 --> 00:15:41.000
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

337
00:15:41.000 --> 00:15:43.018
<v MONTGOMERY>All I know he left.

338
00:15:43.018 --> 00:15:45.035
He went to the priest.

339
00:15:45.035 --> 00:15:46.649
Confessed to the priest.

340
00:15:46.649 --> 00:15:49.877
Which he hadn't been in over forty-some years.

341
00:15:49.877 --> 00:15:51.895
He went to the priest.

342
00:15:51.895 --> 00:15:57.140
And when my daddy died the priest told everybody in church . . . Packed house.

343
00:15:57.140 --> 00:15:59.158
You couldn't get in there.

344
00:15:59.158 --> 00:16:02.789
He said, "Truly he was a man of God."

345
00:16:02.789 --> 00:16:03.193
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

346
00:16:03.193 --> 00:16:08.035
<v MONTGOMERY>They couldn't wake him like they wake people like me or you.

347
00:16:08.035 --> 00:16:09.649
They couldn't wake him.

348
00:16:09.649 --> 00:16:13.684
They had to wake him twenty-four hours around the clock.

349
00:16:13.684 --> 00:16:14.895
<v DIGILORMO>So many people?

350
00:16:14.895 --> 00:16:18.123
<v MONTGOMERY>They had to wake him night and day.

351
00:16:18.123 --> 00:16:18.930
Oh, yeah.

352
00:16:18.930 --> 00:16:24.175
White and black went there crying on their knees when that man died.

353
00:16:24.175 --> 00:16:24.579
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

354
00:16:24.579 --> 00:16:27.000
Was your whole family Catholic?

355
00:16:27.000 --> 00:16:28.714
<v MONTGOMERY>Oh, yeah, all Catholic.

356
00:16:28.714 --> 00:16:29.571
<v DIGILORMO>All Catholic.

357
00:16:29.571 --> 00:16:33.000
Now did your family speak French at all?

358
00:16:33.000 --> 00:16:36.000
<v MONTGOMERY>No. We learned that on our own.

359
00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:46.286
See my daddy, he was a treater and the white folks would come home. [speaking in French] Well we do the best we can.

360
00:16:46.286 --> 00:16:48.429
And that's how we learned.

361
00:16:48.429 --> 00:16:51.000
<v DIGILORMO>What did you just say?

362
00:16:51.000 --> 00:16:52.138
<v MONTGOMERY>Where's your daddy?

363
00:16:52.138 --> 00:16:53.276
<v DIGILORMO>Where's your daddy?

364
00:16:53.276 --> 00:16:54.793
<v MONTGOMERY>Where has he gone?

365
00:16:54.793 --> 00:16:57.069
When he going to be back?

366
00:16:57.069 --> 00:16:57.828
<v DIGILORMO>You . . . [Laughs]

367
00:16:57.828 --> 00:16:59.345
<v MONTGOMERY>Where has he gone?

368
00:16:59.345 --> 00:17:02.000
<v DIGILORMO>You would know those sentences. [Laughs]

369
00:17:02.000 --> 00:17:02.817
<v MONTGOMERY>You know?

370
00:17:02.817 --> 00:17:05.268
And that's what they'd tell us.

371
00:17:05.268 --> 00:17:06.493
They were still

372
00:17:06.493 --> 00:17:06.901
<c.p>Frenchmen.

373
00:17:06.901 --> 00:17:07.310
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

374
00:17:07.310 --> 00:17:09.352
<v MONTGOMERY>And that's how we learned.

375
00:17:09.352 --> 00:17:13.437
Because our daddy and mother didn't speak French to us.

376
00:17:13.437 --> 00:17:15.070
<v DIGILORMO>But he knew it?

377
00:17:15.070 --> 00:17:18.746
<v MONTGOMERY>Oh, they knew it, but they wouldn't talk it.

378
00:17:18.746 --> 00:17:20.380
They talked just English.

379
00:17:20.380 --> 00:17:26.507
But people come all day long. [Speaks French] Well we do the best we can.

380
00:17:26.507 --> 00:17:26.915
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

381
00:17:26.915 --> 00:17:31.000
So people were coming and asking you those questions?

382
00:17:31.000 --> 00:17:31.385
<v MONTGOMERY>Yeah.

383
00:17:31.385 --> 00:17:32.154
<v DIGILORMO>I understand.

384
00:17:32.154 --> 00:17:36.000
<v MONTGOMERY>Yeah, and we had to do the best we could.

385
00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:36.385
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

386
00:17:36.385 --> 00:17:40.231
<v MONTGOMERY>We didn't know what we were saying, but we learned.

387
00:17:40.231 --> 00:17:41.000
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

388
00:17:41.000 --> 00:17:41.428
<v MONTGOMERY>Yeah.

389
00:17:41.428 --> 00:17:42.283
We learned.

390
00:17:42.283 --> 00:17:49.553
And became now, still is . . . Thank Jesus I still got that French [?] but . . . And so that night,

391
00:17:49.553 --> 00:17:55.112
when we was in that night club, my brother Walter, "Wes," he said.

392
00:17:55.112 --> 00:17:58.533
"You lost Dad's treatment, because of one reason."

393
00:17:58.533 --> 00:17:59.816
I said, "Why?"

394
00:17:59.816 --> 00:18:05.375
"Wes I never seen a man with so many women in my life."

395
00:18:05.375 --> 00:18:07.941
He say, "Too many women, Wes."

396
00:18:07.941 --> 00:18:09.224
Which was right.

397
00:18:09.224 --> 00:18:12.645
"Wes," he say, "You have so many women.

398
00:18:12.645 --> 00:18:16.066
Wes, I never seen a man with women.

399
00:18:16.066 --> 00:18:16.921
You know?

400
00:18:16.921 --> 00:18:21.197
White, black, you had them all over the world, Wes."

401
00:18:21.197 --> 00:18:26.329
He says, "You . . ." He says, "That's why you didn't got that treatment."

402
00:18:26.329 --> 00:18:29.750
So I thought about this a long time.

403
00:18:29.750 --> 00:18:32.316
So I told him one day.

404
00:18:32.316 --> 00:18:36.592
I said, "Walter,” I said, “you know, you probably right."

405
00:18:36.592 --> 00:18:41.724
But I did have a lot of women . . . They were beautiful girls.

406
00:18:41.724 --> 00:18:46.000
<v DIGILORMO>Now how old were you whenever your father passed?

407
00:18:46.000 --> 00:18:49.617
<v MONTGOMERY>When my daddy passed, honey, hell, I was married then.

408
00:18:49.617 --> 00:18:51.064
I had four children.

409
00:18:51.064 --> 00:18:51.426
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

410
00:18:51.426 --> 00:18:53.234
So you were in Mossville?

411
00:18:53.234 --> 00:18:53.596
<v MONTGOMERY>Yes.

412
00:18:53.596 --> 00:18:55.404
Oh, I was in Mossville.

413
00:18:55.404 --> 00:18:55.766
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

414
00:18:55.766 --> 00:18:57.213
<v MONTGOMERY>That's where I stayed.

415
00:18:57.213 --> 00:18:57.574
<v DIGILORMO>Right.

416
00:18:57.574 --> 00:19:00.106
<v MONTGOMERY>Since the age of fourteen years old.

417
00:19:00.106 --> 00:19:00.468
<v DIGILORMO>Right.

418
00:19:00.468 --> 00:19:03.000
When you moved with your brother?

419
00:19:03.000 --> 00:19:04.806
<v MONTGOMERY>With Valery, my brother.

420
00:19:04.806 --> 00:19:05.257
<v DIGILORMO>Valery.

421
00:19:05.257 --> 00:19:07.063
<v MONTGOMERY>That's where I stayed.

422
00:19:07.063 --> 00:19:12.481
And when my brother told me that, it really got to me.

423
00:19:12.481 --> 00:19:16.093
So I . . . I says, "I guess you're right."

424
00:19:16.093 --> 00:19:20.607
But I did . . . I did have a lot of girls.

425
00:19:20.607 --> 00:19:28.734
I . . . white girls, black girls, they come in . . . Some of them flew two thousand miles from Los Angeles.

426
00:19:28.734 --> 00:19:30.991
Went to Howard Johnson Motel.

427
00:19:30.991 --> 00:19:32.797
Paid for the room.

428
00:19:32.797 --> 00:19:34.603
Paid for the motel.

429
00:19:34.603 --> 00:19:38.214
Just to come with me to make love.

430
00:19:38.214 --> 00:19:40.020
I was that good.

431
00:19:40.020 --> 00:19:40.472
<v DIGILORMO>Wow.

432
00:19:40.472 --> 00:19:44.084
<v MONTGOMERY>I was good at what I was doing.

433
00:19:44.084 --> 00:19:44.986
I was.

434
00:19:44.986 --> 00:19:48.147
I was just like a bred stallion.

435
00:19:48.147 --> 00:19:49.050
You know?

436
00:19:49.050 --> 00:19:54.016
Yeah and then the world knew it, and everybody knew it.

437
00:19:54.016 --> 00:19:58.982
My boss man told me . . . Chester Barrett was the same age.

438
00:19:58.982 --> 00:20:02.142
And I was driving this big equipment.

439
00:20:02.142 --> 00:20:03.497
He called me.

440
00:20:03.497 --> 00:20:04.400
I had

441
00:20:04.400 --> 00:20:08.011
<c.p>went and buried my brother in Los Angeles.

442
00:20:08.011 --> 00:20:08.463
A.B.

443
00:20:08.463 --> 00:20:08.914
Montgomery.

444
00:20:08.914 --> 00:20:09.366
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

445
00:20:09.366 --> 00:20:11.172
<v MONTGOMERY>Went and buried him.

446
00:20:11.172 --> 00:20:18.395
So I met a girl in Los Angeles, and the girl fell in love with me.

447
00:20:18.395 --> 00:20:21.555
Well, I had to come back home.

448
00:20:21.555 --> 00:20:27.876
I can’t stay with her . . . I got a wife, and I got five children.

449
00:20:27.876 --> 00:20:32.842
So I come back home, and I'm driving that big machine.

450
00:20:32.842 --> 00:20:38.260
And I had a walkie-talkie and he said, "Wes, this Chester Barrett."

451
00:20:38.260 --> 00:20:39.614
That's my boss.

452
00:20:39.614 --> 00:20:44.580
He said, "You need to come to the office right away."

453
00:20:44.580 --> 00:20:50.449
"Chester," I said, "I'm [ . . .?]" He said, "Wes, I need you to come in."

454
00:20:50.449 --> 00:20:52.707
"Chester," I said, "I can't."

455
00:20:52.707 --> 00:20:59.027
"Wes," that's my boss now, a white guy, "You need to come here now."

456
00:20:59.027 --> 00:21:03.993
So I had to tell my people that I'm working with.

457
00:21:03.993 --> 00:21:09.411
I said, "I'm sorry, shut it down." [ . . .?] I got to his office.

458
00:21:09.411 --> 00:21:12.120
He gave me a phone number.

459
00:21:12.120 --> 00:21:15.731
He said, "I'm going to lock the door."

460
00:21:15.731 --> 00:21:18.440
He said, "You call this number."

461
00:21:18.440 --> 00:21:22.955
I says, "Chester, I'm . . ." "Wes," he said, "call this number."

462
00:21:22.955 --> 00:21:29.275
So he got [. . . ?] he locked the doors and said, “Don’t let nobody in here.”

463
00:21:29.275 --> 00:21:31.533
It was Howard Johnson Motel.

464
00:21:31.533 --> 00:21:35.144
They done change it five, six times now.

465
00:21:35.144 --> 00:21:41.465
Them foreign people got . . . But that time it was Howard Johnson on Highway 14.

466
00:21:41.465 --> 00:21:44.625
You can go look in the books.

467
00:21:44.625 --> 00:21:47.334
That was the original Howard Johnson.

468
00:21:47.334 --> 00:21:49.140
I dialed that number.

469
00:21:49.140 --> 00:21:52.752
She pick up that phone, she say, "Hello."

470
00:21:52.752 --> 00:21:55.912
I said, "Who am I speaking to?"

471
00:21:55.912 --> 00:21:57.266
She told me.

472
00:21:57.266 --> 00:21:58.621
She said, "Wes."

473
00:21:58.621 --> 00:22:01.330
I said, "What you doing here?"

474
00:22:01.330 --> 00:22:04.490
She said, "I can't live without you."

475
00:22:04.490 --> 00:22:07.199
I said, "You two thousand miles."

476
00:22:07.199 --> 00:22:10.810
She says, "Wes, I'm here for a month."

477
00:22:10.810 --> 00:22:13.971
I said, "You came two thousand miles."

478
00:22:13.971 --> 00:22:17.131
She said, "I done paid the motel.

479
00:22:17.131 --> 00:22:19.840
I got round trip airfare. [ . . .?] everything.”

480
00:22:19.840 --> 00:22:21.646
About twenty-six years old.

481
00:22:21.646 --> 00:22:23.000
Well educated.

482
00:22:23.000 --> 00:22:25.179
<v DIGILORMO>And she knew you were married?

